{"id": "tc_076", "prompt": "Design a high-level software architecture for a distributed rate limiter supporting 100,000 requests per second. Include components, caching layer, storage, and handling race conditions in 4 bullet points.", "expected_output": "1. Edge API Gateway with Redis cache cluster running Sliding Window counter algorithm.\n2. Use Redis Lua scripts to execute atomic operations and avoid race conditions.\n3. Asynchronous fallback logger to persistent DB (Cassandra/DynamoDB) for audit logs.\n4. Distributed lock or local token bucket algorithm for graceful degraded operation during cache partition.", "system_prompt": "You are a principal systems architect."},
  {"id": "tc_077", "prompt": "Analyze the potential edge cases and security risks of implementing JWT authentication stored exclusively in `localStorage` vs HTTP-Only Secure Cookies. Detail 3 major differences.", "expected_output": "1. XSS Vulnerability: LocalStorage is accessible via JavaScript, exposing JWTs to Cross-Site Scripting; HTTP-only cookies prevent JS access.\n2. CSRF Vulnerability: HTTP-Only cookies are automatically attached by browsers, opening CSRF risk unless SameSite=Strict and anti-CSRF tokens are enforced.\n3. Expiration & Revocation: LocalStorage tokens require explicit client-side removal or server token-blacklisting; HTTP-Only cookie strategies can leverage SameSite policies.", "system_prompt": "You are a cybersecurity expert."},
  {"id": "tc_078", "prompt": "Write a thread-safe Singleton pattern implementation in Python using double-checked locking mechanism.", "expected_output": "import threading\n\nclass Singleton:\n    _instance = None\n    _lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def __new__(cls):\n        if cls._instance is None:\n            with cls._lock:\n                if cls._instance is None:\n                    cls._instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        return cls._instance", "system_prompt": "You are a Python software architect."},
  {"id": "tc_079", "prompt": "A company wants to migrate a monolithic legacy system to microservices. Explain the Strangler Fig Pattern, how to implement it gradually, and how to route traffic using an API Gateway.", "expected_output": "The Strangler Fig Pattern incrementally replaces legacy monolithic components with microservices by intercepting incoming requests at an API Gateway layer. Over time, route specific API paths from the monolith to newly deployed microservices until the monolith is completely decoupled and deprecated without big-bang migration risk.", "system_prompt": "You are a cloud migration architect."},
  {"id": "tc_080", "prompt": "Derive the closed-form time complexity and space complexity of the Merge Sort algorithm using the Master Theorem for divide-and-conquer recurrences: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n).", "expected_output": "For T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n), Master Theorem case 2 applies (a=2, b=2, f(n)=n). Since n^(log_b(a)) = n^1 = n, T(n) = O(n log n). Space complexity is O(n) due to auxiliary arrays required during the merge step.", "system_prompt": "You are a theoretical computer science professor."},
  {"id": "tc_081", "prompt": "Explain the concept of CAP Theorem in distributed databases. Detail why a system cannot simultaneously guarantee Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance during a network failure.", "expected_output": "CAP Theorem states a distributed store can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance. When a network partition (P) occurs, the system must choose between cancelling the operation and decreasing Availability (CP) or proceeding with stale data and sacrificing Consistency (AP).", "system_prompt": "You are a distributed systems researcher."},
  {"id": "tc_082", "prompt": "Draft a legal disclaimer clause for an AI application informing users that model outputs may contain inaccuracies and should not be taken as professional legal or medical advice.", "expected_output": "DISCLAIMER: The information provided by this application is generated by artificial intelligence and is intended for general informational purposes only. It does not constitute legal, medical, or financial advice. Users should independently verify all information with qualified professionals prior to taking action.", "system_prompt": "You are a corporate legal counsel assistant."},
  {"id": "tc_083", "prompt": "Compare and contrast SQL (Relational) vs NoSQL (Document/Key-Value) databases across 4 specific dimensions: Schema flexibility, Scalability model, ACID compliance, and Query complexity.", "expected_output": "1. Schema: SQL uses rigid pre-defined tables; NoSQL uses flexible schema-less documents/JSON.\n2. Scalability: SQL scales vertically (hardware capacity); NoSQL scales horizontally (sharding across nodes).\n3. ACID: SQL natively guarantees strict ACID transactions; NoSQL prioritizes BASE consistency model.\n4. Queries: SQL uses expressive declarative queries; NoSQL relies on primary key lookups or map-reduce patterns.", "system_prompt": "You are a principal database administrator."},
  {"id": "tc_084", "prompt": "Write a complete C++ code snippet that implements a custom Memory Pool Allocator for fixed-size memory blocks.", "expected_output": "template<typename T, size_t BlockCount>\nclass MemoryPool {\n    union Node { T element; Node* next; };\n    Node* freeList = nullptr;\n    char storage[sizeof(Node) * BlockCount];\npublic:\n    MemoryPool() {\n        Node* pool = reinterpret_cast<Node*>(storage);\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < BlockCount - 1; ++i) pool[i].next = &pool[i + 1];\n        pool[BlockCount - 1].next = nullptr;\n        freeList = pool;\n    }\n    T* allocate() {\n        if (!freeList) throw std::bad_alloc();\n        Node* node = freeList;\n        freeList = freeList->next;\n        return &(node->element);\n    }\n    void deallocate(T* ptr) {\n        Node* node = reinterpret_cast<Node*>(ptr);\n        node->next = freeList;\n        freeList = node;\n    }\n};", "system_prompt": "You are a C++ systems programmer."},
  {"id": "tc_085", "prompt": "Explain the zero-knowledge proof concept (ZKP) using the 'Ali Baba Cave' analogy in 3 concise paragraphs.", "expected_output": "Zero-Knowledge Proofs allow a Prover to demonstrate knowledge of a secret to a Verifier without revealing the secret itself.\n\nIn the Ali Baba cave analogy, a cave has two branching paths connected by a magic door opened only by a secret password. The Verifier stands outside while the Prover enters a random branch. The Verifier then calls out a exit path (A or B). If the Prover knows the secret, they can open the door and exit whichever path called.\n\nBy repeating this process multiple times, the probability of guessing correctly drops exponentially, proving knowledge without revealing the secret password.", "system_prompt": "You are a cryptography professor."},
  {"id": "tc_086", "prompt": "Write an asynchronous worker queue in Python using `asyncio.Queue` that consumes tasks from 3 concurrent worker tasks and handles graceful shutdown.", "expected_output": "import asyncio\n\nasync def worker(name, queue):\n    while True:\n        task = await queue.get()\n        if task is None: break\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.1)\n        queue.task_done()\n\nasync def main():\n    queue = asyncio.Queue()\n    workers = [asyncio.create_task(worker(f'w-{i}', queue)) for i in range(3)]\n    for i in range(10): await queue.put(i)\n    await queue.join()\n    for _ in workers: await queue.put(None)\n    await asyncio.gather(*workers)", "system_prompt": "You are a Python concurrency engineer."},
  {"id": "tc_087", "prompt": "Synthesize the core mechanisms of Transformer neural network architectures: Explain Self-Attention, Multi-Head Attention, and Positional Encoding.", "expected_output": "1. Self-Attention: Calculates query, key, and value matrices (Q, K, V) to weigh the relevance of every token relative to all other tokens in a sequence.\n2. Multi-Head Attention: Runs multiple attention mechanisms in parallel, allowing the model to attend to information from different representation subspaces simultaneously.\n3. Positional Encoding: Adds sinusoidal vectors to input embeddings to inject positional sequence order since Transformers lack inherent recurrence.", "system_prompt": "You are an AI research scientist."},
  {"id": "tc_088", "prompt": "Write a Kubernetes deployment YAML manifest configuring a deployment named `web-app` with 3 replicas, resource limits (256Mi memory, 500m CPU), and a readiness probe.", "expected_output": "apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: web-app\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web-app\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: web-app\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: web\n        image: nginx:latest\n        resources:\n          limits:\n            memory: \"256Mi\"\n            cpu: \"500m\"\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /healthz\n            port: 80", "system_prompt": "You are a Kubernetes infrastructure engineer."},
  {"id": "tc_089", "prompt": "Evaluate the financial trade-offs between Cloud IaaS deployment (AWS/Azure) vs On-Premises bare metal infrastructure for a high-growth SaaS business over a 5-year timeline.", "expected_output": "Cloud IaaS offers lower initial CapEx, rapid scalability, and managed operational overhead, but incurs higher long-term OpEx at sustained scale. On-Premises bare metal requires substantial upfront CapEx and specialized infrastructure teams, but achieves significantly lower per-unit computing costs and higher margin predictability at predictable, steady-state enterprise volumes.", "system_prompt": "You are a CFO and cloud infrastructure advisor."},
  {"id": "tc_090", "prompt": "Construct a prompt template using Few-Shot Learning and Chain-of-Thought prompting to guide an LLM to accurately solve multi-step financial word problems.", "expected_output": "System: Solve the financial problem by thinking step-by-step before producing the final numerical answer.\n\nExample:\nQ: A portfolio worth $10,000 grows by 10% in Year 1 and loses 10% in Year 2. What is the final value?\nA: Step 1: Calculate Year 1 value = $10,000 * 1.10 = $11,000.\nStep 2: Calculate Year 2 value = $11,000 * 0.90 = $9,900.\nFinal Answer: 9900.\n\nQ: [INSERT USER QUESTION HERE]\nA:", "system_prompt": "You are a prompt engineering specialist."},
  {"id": "tc_091", "prompt": "Explain raft consensus algorithm state transitions (Follower, Candidate, Leader) and how leader election prevents split-brain scenarios.", "expected_output": "Nodes begin as Followers. If heartbeat times out, a Follower converts to Candidate, increments term, and requests votes. Receiving majority votes converts it to Leader.\nSplit-brain is prevented because a candidate MUST secure a strict majority (N/2 + 1) of node votes to become Leader. Since each node can vote for at most one candidate per term, two disjoint majorities cannot exist concurrently.", "system_prompt": "You are a distributed systems architect."},
  {"id": "tc_092", "prompt": "Write a Python script using `pydantic` to validate a complex nested user payload containing `user_id` (UUID), `email` (EmailStr), and `address` (nested model with street, zip code regex).", "expected_output": "from pydantic import BaseModel, EmailStr, Field\nfrom uuid import UUID\n\nclass Address(BaseModel):\n    street: str\n    zip_code: str = Field(..., pattern=r'^\\d{5}(-\\d{4})?$')\n\nclass UserPayload(BaseModel):\n    user_id: UUID\n    email: EmailStr\n    address: Address", "system_prompt": "You are a Python data engineer."},
  {"id": "tc_093", "prompt": "Describe how Garbage Collection works in Java (Generational GC: Young, Old, Metaspace) and distinguish G1GC from ZGC/Shenandoah.", "expected_output": "Java Generational GC splits heap into Young Generation (Eden/Survivors for short-lived objects) and Tenured/Old Generation (promoted long-lived objects). Minor GC cleans Young, Major/Full GC cleans Old.\nTraditional G1GC partitions heap into regions balancing throughput and pause targets, whereas modern low-latency collectors (ZGC/Shenandoah) perform concurrent marking and compaction to maintain sub-millisecond pause times regardless of heap size.", "system_prompt": "You are a JVM performance tuning engineer."},
  {"id": "tc_094", "prompt": "Provide a comprehensive code solution in Python to detect cycles in a directed graph using Depth-First Search (DFS) with 3 node color states (WHITE, GRAY, BLACK).", "expected_output": "def has_cycle(graph):\n    visited = {node: 'WHITE' for node in graph}\n    def dfs(node):\n        visited[node] = 'GRAY'\n        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):\n            if visited[neighbor] == 'GRAY': return True\n            if visited[neighbor] == 'WHITE' and dfs(neighbor): return True\n        visited[node] = 'BLACK'\n        return False\n    return any(dfs(node) for node in graph if visited[node] == 'WHITE')", "system_prompt": "You are a computer science instructor."},
  {"id": "tc_095", "prompt": "Explain the mechanics of a Spectre side-channel attack (Speculative Execution & Cache Timing) and how modern microprocessors mitigate it.", "expected_output": "Spectre exploits speculative execution, where CPUs speculatively execute instructions past branch boundaries. If speculation fails, architectural state rolls back, but transient memory access leaves microarchitectural traces in CPU L1 cache. Attackers measure cache access timing (Flush+Reload) to infer secret data.\nMitigations include speculative barrier instructions (LFENCE), hardware branch predictor isolation, and software browser timer reduction.", "system_prompt": "You are a hardware security researcher."},
  {"id": "tc_096", "prompt": "Outline an end-to-end MLOps pipeline strategy for continuous retraining and deployment of an LLM embedding model, including data drift monitoring and automated rollback triggers.", "expected_output": "1. Ingestion & Monitoring: Track vector drift and embedding retrieval latency using statistical metrics (KS-test, cosine similarity decay).\n2. Automated Retraining: Trigger Airflow/Prefect pipelines to fine-tune on refreshed data upon drift threshold breach.\n3. Evaluation & Shadow Deployment: Run automated evaluation benchmark harness against golden datasets; deploy shadow traffic to compare candidate vs baseline.\n4. Canary Deployment & Rollback: Gradually shift production traffic (10% -> 100%); automatically roll back via CI/CD if error rates elevate.", "system_prompt": "You are an MLOps platform lead."},
  {"id": "tc_097", "prompt": "Write a complete SQL CTE (Common Table Expression) query to compute a running 7-day moving average of sales per store from a `daily_sales` table.", "expected_output": "WITH DailyStoreSales AS (\n    SELECT store_id, sale_date, SUM(amount) AS daily_total\n    FROM daily_sales\n    GROUP BY store_id, sale_date\n)\nSELECT store_id, sale_date, daily_total,\n       AVG(daily_total) OVER (\n           PARTITION BY store_id \n           ORDER BY sale_date \n           ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n       ) AS moving_avg_7d\nFROM DailyStoreSales;", "system_prompt": "You are a senior data warehouse engineer."},
  {"id": "tc_098", "prompt": "Write a Rust function signature and implementation for `safe_divide(a: f64, b: f64) -> Result<f64, String>` handling division by zero.", "expected_output": "fn safe_divide(a: f64, b: f64) -> Result<f64, String> {\n    if b == 0.0 {\n        Err(String::from(\"Division by zero\"))\n    } else {\n        Ok(a / b)\n    }\n}", "system_prompt": "You are a Rust programming instructor."},
  {"id": "tc_099", "prompt": "Explain the concept of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), detailing Document Chunking, Vector Embedding, Hybrid Search (Dense + Sparse), and Reranking.", "expected_output": "RAG enhances LLM responses by fetching relevant external knowledge context before generation.\n1. Chunking: Splits documents into semantically coherent text segments.\n2. Vector Embedding: Converts chunks into high-dimensional vector representations.\n3. Hybrid Search: Combines Dense Search (semantic vector similarity) with Sparse Search (BM25 keyword matching) for high recall.\n4. Reranking: Uses a cross-encoder model to re-score and select top-k chunks for context injection into the LLM prompt.", "system_prompt": "You are an AI solutions architect."},
  {"id": "tc_100", "prompt": "Write a Python script using standard `asyncio` and `aiohttp` (or `urllib.request`) to perform asynchronous concurrent HTTP GET requests across a list of 5 URLs with timeout handling.", "expected_output": "import asyncio\nimport urllib.request\n\nasync def fetch_url(url):\n    loop = asyncio.get_event_loop()\n    try:\n        req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0'})\n        res = await loop.run_in_executor(None, lambda: urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=3).read())\n        return len(res)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return str(e)\n\nasync def main(urls):\n    tasks = [fetch_url(url) for url in urls]\n    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)", "system_prompt": "You are an asynchronous Python developer."}
]
 

  

  
